id: rust.traits
kind: rust
title: Traits
aliases: traits, trait bounds, impl Trait
tags: fundamentals, api design
questions:
  - how do traits differ from interfaces?
invariant: Traits define behaviour in terms of the implementer; generic bounds monomorphise, dyn Trait dispatches through a vtable.
answer_30s: Traits describe behaviour; taking them as bounds gives static dispatch with no allocation, while dyn Trait gives runtime polymorphism at the cost of indirection.
answer_2m: |
  The design decision is dispatch. Generics plus bounds (or `impl Trait`)
  monomorphise: fastest, but the concrete type must be known at compile time and
  code size grows. `Box<dyn Trait>` allows heterogeneous collections and
  runtime-selected implementations with one code path. Server code typically
  wants generics at internal seams and dyn at boundaries where the backend is
  chosen at startup (repositories, embedders, transports). Remember the orphan
  rule: you can implement a trait only if you own the trait or the type, which is
  why newtype wrappers exist.
when_to_use:
  - abstraction seams, pluggable backends, test doubles
minimal_example: |
  trait Store { fn get(&self, k: &str) -> Option<String>; }

  fn read_static<S: Store>(s: &S, k: &str) -> Option<String> { s.get(k) }
  fn read_dynamic(s: &dyn Store, k: &str) -> Option<String> { s.get(k) }
failure_modes:
  - boxed dyn in a hot path, paying indirection without needing polymorphism
  - over-generic functions, so every call site compiles a new body
related:
  - rust.generics-monomorphization
  - rust.dyn-vs-generic
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-02-traits.html
